VDB maintenance involves using documented database upgrade procedures. You can disable your VDB and then perform a standard database upgrade against it. 
    use cases:  
        Upgrading a VDB independently from the Source DB

        Refreshing the VDB after the Source DB is upgraded


Workflow for independently upgrading a VDB:
    Put new “brain” on the machine
    Install the new database software (new version) on the target server where the VDB lives.

    Do the normal upgrade stuff
    Use the database’s usual upgrade/patch steps (Oracle/SQL Server etc.) on that server, just like you would for a real database.

    Let Delphix re‑look at the machine
    In Delphix, refresh the target environment so it can “see” the new database installation and services you just added.

    Tell the VDB to use the new version
    Disable/stop the VDB.
    Use the Upgrade option to point the VDB’s installation/repository to the new database version.

Workflow for Refreshing a VDB:
    Think of a VDB as a toy copy of a big Lego city (the source database).

    Upgrade the real city first
    You change and upgrade the real Lego city (the source database) using its normal upgrade steps.

    Put the new Lego pieces on the toy table
    Install the new database software (new binaries) on the target server where the VDB lives, so it can understand the upgraded city.

    Let Delphix take a fresh look around
    In Delphix, you refresh the target environment so it “sees” the new database installation and services you just added.

    Tell the VDB to use the new tools

    Stop/disable the VDB.
    Use the Upgrade option so the VDB now points to the new database version (same as the upgraded source).
    Give the toy city a fresh copy of the upgraded world

    In Delphix, choose Refresh VDB.
    Pick a new snapshot from the source that was taken after the source was upgraded.
    Delphix then “rebuilds” the VDB from this upgraded snapshot.
    End result:

    First, the real city gets upgraded.
    Then the toy copy (VDB) is refreshed so it matches that new upgraded city.

Updating the Oracle_installation:
    On the VDB Configuration tab, click the stop icon to stop the VDB.

    From the Actions menu (...) select upgrade to switch the ORACLE_INSTALLATION to the new upgrade version same as the dSource.

Refresh the VDB:
    Navigate to the Datasets view, select the VDB, and then click the Timeflow tab.

    Click the Refresh button.

    Select a new snapshot from the dSource that was taken after the dSource was upgraded. Choosing Faster will use the most recent dSource snapshot.


VDB maintenance involves using documented database upgrade procedures. You can disable your VDB and then perform a standard database upgrade against it. 

two use cases associated with upgrading a VDB:
    1. Upgrading a VDB independently from the source DB
    2.Refreshing the VDB after the source DB is upgraded 

“Independently upgrading a VDB” = upgrade the VDB itself without upgrading the source.

Very simply, the workflow is:

    Install the new database version/binaries on the target server where the VDB lives.

    Run the normal database upgrade steps on that server (as if it were a physical DB).

    In Delphix, refresh the target environment so Delphix discovers the new installation.

    Disable/stop the VDB, then use the Upgrade action to point the VDB to that new database installation/version.

    Result: the VDB now runs on the new database version, independently of its source.

Migrating a VBD:
    when upgrading the host on which the VDB resides, or as part of a general data center migration. This is easily accomplished by first disabling the database, then using the Migrate VDB feature to select a new target environment.

 










